=== 4. Findings

L1 (blocking). The tolerance diff.json states is not the tolerance the test
applies.
  - mathd/fixtures/generate.py lines 889-890 write
    "tolerance_relative": 1e-10 and "zero_tolerance_absolute": 0.0 into
    diff.json. Section 3 prints that header.
  - mathd/tests/differentiator.rs lines 331-333 apply
    1e-10 * max(1, |want|). Below |want| = 1 that is an absolute 1e-10,
    not a relative one. Nothing asserts the header against the test.
  - Consequence: the 18 cases with 0 < |want| < 1e-10 (listed in section 3)
    check nothing. For each one, got = 0 and got = -want both pass, because
    |want| itself is below 1e-10. The 10 cases between 1e-10 and 1e-6 allow
    relative errors from 1 down to 1e-4.
  - The strict rule is reachable: on HEAD it fails one case of 766 (section
    2), d/dz sin((exp(z)/65.51)(20z)) at z = -1. The true value is exactly 0,
    because the chain factor 20 e^z (1 + z)/65.51 is 0 at z = -1; f64 gives
    -1.379e-17 from cancellation. The 18 small cases all pass the strict
    rule.
  - This is the same class as B3 F2 and F3 (docs/reviews/b3-claude.log),
    which the author fixed for eval.json: tests/evaluator.rs lines 272-286
    assert the header against the test's constants. diff.json did not get
    the same treatment.
  Fix (the author picks the rule; the stated rule and the applied rule must
  be the same):
    (a) make the header state the rule the test applies, in words a reader
        can check, and assert the header against the test's constants as
        tests/evaluator.rs does; and
    (b) make the cases with |want| < 1e-10 count. Either the strict
        relative rule (then deal with the one cancellation case the way B3
        did: a generator filter that prints its discard count and reason),
        or a stated floor with its reason, plus a count of the cases the
        floor makes vacuous.
  Show the test failing on a planted fault that only the new rule sees, for
  example negating the derivative's value when |value| < 1e-10 in a scratch
  copy, as B3 break 11 did.

L2 (not blocking). The author's CFD counts (48,254 tested, 1,746 skipped)
are still not reproduced. docs/logs/b4-ci-38788817078.log shows only that
the test passed, because cargo captures println! (tests/differentiator.rs
line 592). freebuff's independent CFD loop gives 48,018 / 1,982 with a
different generator, so the scale agrees but the numbers are not the
author's. Either print them in CI (`cargo test --test differentiator --
--nocapture`) and commit that log, or drop the numbers from progress.md.

L3 (not blocking). The exponential rule has 3 fixture cases (2^x, 3^x,
pi^x) and the general power rule has 2 (x^x, (x + 1)^x). D1-D3 are caught,
but by those cases only; the CFD generator uses constant integer exponents
(freebuff, note 2). Add more of both, for example in B6's corpus.

Note on freebuff's probe (b). A derivative that fails to evaluate is counted
as a skip (tests/differentiator.rs lines 552-557), bounded only by
skip_count < total/2 (line 605). freebuff showed a quotient-rule break that
the CFD test alone passes at a 10.81% skip rate; the fixtures catch it. I
agree it is not a B4 defect today. It becomes one if the fixtures ever stop
covering a rule.

